{
 "_comment": "IAW 5AM live-data map. Channel names are joined to their rli by the local channel id. Cylinders labelled F/R per the V-twin layout; this engine's V-angle is 87 degrees, so the firing interval is uneven (87/633) and no per-cylinder pair should be expected to sit a fixed 90 apart. Response '61 <rli> <value>', offset 2, big-endian; length per rli from the 2026-08-18 scan. Two flags, two jobs: 'group' says which list the UI draws the row in -- 'known' = proven on this bike, 'check' = named but still to be proven there, 'unknown' = no meaning yet -- while 'default' says what a device with no selected.json polls out of the box. The 2026-09-04 stationary session moved five channels into 'known': 0x39 is the injection period at raw/1000 ms (0 with the engine off, 8.6 ms cranking, 1.89 ms at 36 C), 0x60 is barometric (962 flat, engine on or off), and 0x76/0x78/0x61 are the side stand, the clutch and the neutral flag -- the clutch polarity in status_maps.json had to be swapped, the bike answers 0 with the lever pulled. The 10-minute ride that followed confirmed the lambda integrator's scale 0.1 (-14.4..+16.9 % with 48 zero crossings) and the mV reading of 0x45 (181 crossings of 450 mV), and re-confirmed all three switch polarities from the opposite state -- riding means gear engaged, stand up, lever released. There is no gear number on this ECU: 0x61 is a neutral flag, the 5AM label page has no Value_35 (\"Gear\") slot, and 0x58 -- the one candidate -- turned out to be the engine-state bitfield instead. 0x37 stays open: while running it tracks 0x35 to 0.21 deg, so it is a second advance channel in tenths of a degree, but whether it is the other cylinder or a programmed-advance channel takes a decel/cut-off run to tell -- it reads a flat 8.0 deg with the engine off, which suits 'programmed'. This bike has one lambda sensor, on the front cylinder: the rear is not fitted, so 0x46, 0x48, 0x4A and 0x4C read a flat 0 here and cannot be confirmed on this machine at all -- the names are provisional and the F/R pairing is by rli order. 0x54/0x55 keep the channel but not the word 'angle': across 424 samples the pair settles after every start (869->700 in five seconds from cold, the same from a 40 C and an 87 C start, 1210 while the starter turns) and ignores rpm, which is a charge time compensated for battery voltage, not an angle -- the unit stays raw until a session with vbat and a switched load names it. The 2026-09-05 pair of sessions settled four more. 0x54/0x55 are a charge time compensated for battery voltage: inside a narrow rpm band dwell_f falls monotonically as vbat rises (1200-1600 rpm: 739 at 13.7 V, 737 at 13.8, 723 at 13.9; 2200-3000 rpm: 797 at 13.4, 778 at 13.5, 766 at 13.6), the slope is -80..-150 counts per volt, cranking at 11.2 V reads 1066/2620 and the engine off at 12.3 V a static 731/846, while rpm at a fixed voltage moves it by only ~15 counts per 600 rpm. The unit stays raw -- the count could be ~4 us (2.8 ms at 13.7 V, 4.3 ms cranking) but nothing in the log names the multiplier. 0x6B is the temperature-scheduled base position of the idle stepper: 117 at 51 C, 113 at 60, 108 at 65, 103 at 70, 100 from 75 C up, with idle_target riding along 1472 -> 1404 rpm. 0x49 is the loop flag it was named for: 2 on 3562 samples (lambda swinging about 455 mV), 0 on 947 at small throttle and on the overrun (591 mV mean). 0x58 is confirmed as the engine-state bitfield, but the labels shipped in status_maps.json were wrong and are rewritten there. Still open: 0x37 (the kill-switch run was not made -- it tracks 0x35 to a mean of -0.04 deg with 1.35 deg rms scatter that a regression shows is *not* sampling skew, and holds a static 8.0 deg with the engine stopped while 0x35 reads 0), 0x4B (a phase code: it ping-pongs 7<->5 with the sensor -- 7 at 375 mV with the integrator rising, 5 at 543 mV with it falling -- 3 and 4 on the overrun, 1 and 2 only below 76 C), 0x6D (moved 7..27 with no relation to rpm minus idle_target) and 0x3D/0x72, which read a flat zero across all 4856 samples of both logs.",
 "poll_interval_ms": 150,
 "poll_timeout_ms": 150,
